{"case_id":"T8083","gap_exists":true,"gap_type":["API protocol","state","input semantics"],"confidence":99,"affected_functions":["spng_decode_image","spng_decode_scanline"],"reason":"H0 does not exercise the progressive scanline-decoding protocol implicated by the new per-pass `sub[pass].out_width` bounds check. When `flags` contains `SPNG_DECODE_PROGRESSIVE`, `spng_decode_image()` initializes decoding and returns, but H0 performs no subsequent `spng_decode_scanline()` calls. In non-progressive operation, the internal row loop supplies `ctx->out_width`, so H0 also does not vary scanline buffer lengths around the new required per-pass width.","evidence":["`spng_decode_image()` returns immediately after initialization when `flags & SPNG_DECODE_PROGRESSIVE`.","H0 calls `spng_decode_image(ctx, out, out_size, fmt, flags)` once and then frees `ctx` and `out`; it never calls `spng_decode_scanline()`.","S1 changes `spng_decode_scanline()` from format-dependent checks using `scanline_width - 1` or `width * pixel_size` to `if(len < sub[pass].out_width) return SPNG_EBUFSIZ`.","S1 computes `sub[i].out_width` for each pass from either `sub[i].scanline_width - 1` or `sub[i].width * pixel_size`, making interlace pass, format, and caller-supplied `len` combinations directly relevant.","The non-progressive loop calls `spng_decode_row(ctx, out + ioffset, ctx->out_width)`, preventing H0 from directly testing undersized, exact-sized, and oversized progressive scanline buffers."],"recommended_action":"Add a progressive-decode harness path that calls `spng_decode_image()` with `SPNG_DECODE_PROGRESSIVE`, then repeatedly invokes `spng_decode_scanline()` across interlace passes and formats with fuzz-controlled buffer lengths centered on zero, exact `sub[pass].out_width`, and adjacent undersized/oversized values, while allocating enough backing storage to distinguish API rejection from memory errors."}
